    {
      "title": "Identify the variable being tested",
      "content": "The experiment changes only light exposure, so light is the independent variable and plant growth is the dependent variable. Soil, water, species, and age are controlled variables.",
      "sources": []
    },
    {
      "title": "Explain why controlling variables helps",
      "content": "Holding soil, water, species, and age constant reduces confounding variables. If those factors differed between groups, a difference in growth could be caused by better soil, more water, a different species, or age rather than by light. Controlling them makes it more reasonable to compare growth between light conditions.",
      "sources": []
    },
    {
      "title": "Assess whether one taller plant proves causation",
      "content": "No. One taller plant in the light group alone does not prove that light caused the growth. That result could come from random variation, measurement error, an uncontrolled factor, or chance. To support a causal claim, the experiment should use multiple plants, random assignment, replication, and a statistically reliable difference between groups.",
      "sources": []
    }
  ],
  "answer": "Controlling soil, water, species, and age helps isolate light as the possible cause of growth differences. However, a single taller plant in the light group alone does not prove that light caused growth; replicated controlled experiments and statistical evidence are needed."
}

