    {
      "title": "Assess the letter as a source",
      "content": "No course context is provided, so this step uses general historical method. A letter written by a factory owner is a primary source, but it is also a self-interested account. The owner may have written it to praise the factory, attract customers, defend against criticism, or create a favorable record. It may describe only selected workers, selected conditions, or selected times, and it may omit problems such as low wages, long hours, unsafe machinery, illness, discipline, or resistance.",
      "sources": []
    },
    {
      "title": "Explain why it cannot establish conditions for all workers",
      "content": "One document cannot establish what conditions were like for all workers because it is not independent, not representative, and not complete. It reflects the owner's perspective and purpose, not the experiences of every worker. A favorable letter may be accurate in some details, but by itself it cannot prove that all workers had good conditions, because it does not show what workers said, what official records recorded, or what happened in other departments, shifts, or years.",
      "sources": []
    },
    {
      "title": "Name two kinds of evidence that could test the letter",
      "content": "Two kinds of evidence could strengthen or challenge the account: (1) workers' own evidence, such as diaries, letters, union minutes, petitions, strike records, oral histories, or testimony, which can show whether workers experienced the conditions the owner described; and (2) independent administrative or legal records, such as wage ledgers, inspection reports, accident and illness records, court cases, or newspaper reports, which can confirm or contradict the owner's claims with details about pay, hours, safety, and discipline.",
      "sources": []
    }
  ],
  "answer": "The letter alone cannot establish conditions for all workers because it is a single, self-interested, and potentially selective account by the factory owner. Two kinds of evidence that could strengthen or challenge it are workers' own records or testimony and independent official or legal records such as inspection reports, wage ledgers, accident records, or court documents."
}

